context:
{"patient_information": "A 40-year-old male farmer from the Peruvian Amazon, who was found to be HTLV-1-positive 10 years prior to presentation. A recent HIV ELISA test was negative.", "chief_complaint": "A 4-month history of chronic productive cough and chest pain.", "history_of_present_illness": "The patient presented with a 4-month history of chronic productive cough, chest pain, dyspnoea on exertion, and a weight loss of about 20 kg. He denied diarrhoea, fever, or night sweats. Three and a half months earlier, he was hospitalized for similar complaints and presumptively diagnosed with pulmonary tuberculosis based on clinical and radiographic findings, despite three negative sputum smears for acid-fast bacilli (AFB). He received first-line TB therapy (isoniazid, rifampicin, pyrazinamide, ethambutol) but showed no clinical improvement, with persistence of his productive cough, which later included streaking of blood in the sputum. This led to his referral with a suspected diagnosis of multi-drug resistant TB.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient is a farmer from a rural area in the central Peruvian Amazon. He denies any recent travel.", "vitals": "Temperature was 37.2\u00b0C (98.96\u00b0F), blood pressure was 120/75 mmHg, pulse was 70 bpm regular, and respiratory rate was 15 breaths per minute.", "physical_exam": "The patient appeared fatigued and cachectic. On inspection, a few small, mobile, soft, and non-tender cervical and retroauricular lymph nodes were palpable. Examination of the lungs revealed crackles and rhonchi bilaterally. There was no hepatosplenomegaly, and the rest of the physical examination was normal.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "Initial laboratory results showed a platelet count of 1093 x 10^9/L, indicating thrombocytosis; the rest of the full blood count was within normal limits. Three initial sputum smears for acid-fast bacilli (AFB) were negative, as were two subsequent AFB smears. The tuberculin skin test was also negative. A chest x-ray showed bilateral confluent reticulonodular infiltrates and multiple cavities in the right lung. A definitive diagnosis was made when a sputum KOH preparation revealed the characteristic yeast forms of Paracoccidioides brasiliensis, which was subsequently grown in culture.", "differential_diagnosis": "The primary differential diagnoses considered were multi-drug resistant TB (MDR TB), histoplasmosis, and paracoccidioidomycosis. While TB is endemic in Peru, MDR TB was considered less likely given the patient's origin from a rural area. Histoplasmosis was also less likely as its chronic form is more common in individuals with pre-existing lung disease. Paracoccidioidomycosis was deemed the most probable diagnosis given the patient's age, gender, occupation, origin, and clinical presentation. Less common possibilities included paragonimiasis or pulmonary hydatid cyst.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The patient was initially treated with a full course of first-line anti-tuberculosis therapy for several months without any clinical improvement. After the diagnosis of paracoccidioidomycosis was confirmed, the TB treatment was discontinued. Due to the extensive pulmonary lesions and his HTLV-1 co-infection, he was started on treatment with amphotericin B, to be followed by itraconazole.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis was chronic (adult) paracoccidioidomycosis. This was established by direct visualization of the characteristic yeast forms of Paracoccidioides brasiliensis on a KOH preparation of the patient's sputum, which was subsequently confirmed by fungal culture. The diagnosis was consistent with the patient's epidemiological risk factors (a male farmer from the Peruvian Amazon), chronic pulmonary symptoms with significant weight loss, and failure to respond to empirical anti-tuberculosis therapy.", "disease_name_short": "Paracoccidioidomycosis", "source_filename": "55---A-40-Year-Old-Male-Farmer-from-Peru-With-Ch_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Trop.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "A 52-year-old man with a history of type 2 diabetes mellitus and poorly controlled hypertension.", "chief_complaint": "Abdominal pain and vomiting.", "history_of_present_illness": "The patient presented with a 4-day history of retro-orbital headache, lethargy, myalgia, and fevers. These initial symptoms had begun to improve over the 24 hours prior to presentation, at which point he developed abdominal pain and vomiting.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient lives in an urban hospital setting in Ho Chi Minh City, Vietnam, a high-endemicity area for dengue. He had no history of travel outside the city for several years. The risk of malaria is very low in this urban area, and there was no suspected rodent exposure.", "vitals": "On admission, the patient was afebrile with a temperature of 36.5\u00b0C. He was in compensated shock with a heart rate of 120 bpm, blood pressure of 105/90 mmHg resulting in a narrow pulse pressure, a respiratory rate of 28 breaths per minute, and a peripheral oxygen saturation of 93% on air.", "physical_exam": "The patient was drowsy but rousable with a GCS of 14/15. He had a weak radial pulse. Respiratory examination revealed a dull percussion note and reduced breath sounds at both lung bases. The abdomen showed mild distension with shifting dullness and a tender liver edge palpable 3cm below the costal margin. Skin examination was unremarkable.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "Initial laboratory results showed haemoconcentration with a haematocrit of 53.9%, which later rose to 55.1%. He had leukopenia (WCC 2.1 x10^9/L) and significant thrombocytopenia. C-reactive protein was low (<4 mg/L). There was evidence of shock with a raised serum lactate of 3.5 mmol/L. Ultrasound scans confirmed moderate bilateral pleural effusions, ascites, mild hepatomegaly, and a thickened gallbladder wall. The diagnosis of dengue was confirmed by a positive NS1 ELISA and Reverse Transcriptase PCR (RT-PCR).", "differential_diagnosis": "The primary differential diagnosis was severe dengue with vascular leak and compensated shock, which was considered highly likely given the clinical picture and location. Other important considerations included rickettsial infections like scrub typhus, even without an eschar. Malaria was considered but less likely due to the low urban risk and lack of haemolysis. Leptospirosis was also a possibility, though the absence of rodent exposure made it less probable. Melioidosis was considered given the patient's diabetes and Southeast Asian origin.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The patient was diagnosed with compensated shock and admitted to a high-dependency unit for supportive care. He received careful fluid resuscitation with Ringer\u2019s lactate solution, starting at 10mL/kg over 1 hour and increasing to 15mL/kg/hr due to persistent tachycardia, oliguria, and a rising haematocrit. The fluid rate was subsequently tapered down as his condition improved. He was monitored closely with two-hourly clinical assessments and six-hourly haematocrit checks. The patient made a complete recovery and was discharged from the hospital 3 days later.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis was severe dengue. This was based on the typical clinical progression from a febrile phase to a critical phase at the time of defervescence, characterized by signs of vascular leakage (pleural effusions, ascites, haemoconcentration) and compensated shock. The diagnosis was definitively confirmed by a positive dengue NS1 ELISA and a positive RT-PCR for dengue virus.", "disease_name_short": "Severe Dengue", "source_filename": "9---A-52-Year-Old-Man-from-Vietnam-With-Evo_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Tropical-.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "A 44-year-old male rice farmer from Laos with a history of type 2 diabetes diagnosed 4 years prior. He was reportedly not compliant with his oral antidiabetic medications or follow-up.", "chief_complaint": "A one-month history of fever, headache, generalized myalgia, arthralgia, and a painful swelling over the left scapula.", "history_of_present_illness": "For one month, the patient experienced fever, body aches, and a painful swelling on his left scapula that gradually increased in size without preceding trauma. A week before admission, the lesion was incised and drained at a local health center, and he was started on oral cloxacillin 1g four times daily. Despite this, his condition did not improve. In the two days prior to admission, he deteriorated with high fever, chills, and severe malaise.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient is a rice farmer in Southern Laos and presented during the rainy season. His occupation and geographic location are significant risk factors for melioidosis, which is commonly acquired via percutaneous inoculation through cuts while working in rice fields.", "vitals": "On admission, the patient appeared septic with a fever of 40\u00b0C (104\u00b0F), a heart rate of 136 bpm, blood pressure of 140/80 mmHg, and a respiratory rate of 30 breaths per minute.", "physical_exam": "The patient was pale and jaundiced. There was a 2.5 x 4 cm erythematous swelling in the left scapular region with pus discharging from a central wound. No regional lymphadenopathy was noted. Chest auscultation revealed bilateral crepitations and reduced breath sounds at both lung bases. Heart sounds were normal, and the abdomen was soft without organomegaly.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "Initial laboratory results showed a white blood cell count of 16.2 x10^9/L with 92.1% polymorphs, haemoglobin of 12.5 g/dL, and elevated glucose at 20.6 mmol/L. Liver function tests were abnormal with a total bilirubin of 111.2 \u00b5mol/L and direct bilirubin of 56.4 \u00b5mol/L. Pus culture from the back abscess grew Burkholderia pseudomallei after 48 hours, and blood cultures later grew the same organism. A chest radiograph revealed bilateral pleural effusions. An abdominal ultrasound showed hepatosplenomegaly but no liver or splenic abscesses.", "differential_diagnosis": "The differential diagnosis included a bacterial abscess with sepsis caused by common organisms like Staphylococcus aureus or Streptococcus species. However, given the patient's epidemiological risk factors (diabetic rice farmer from Laos in the rainy season) and clinical presentation, melioidosis caused by Burkholderia pseudomallei was a primary consideration. Tuberculosis was also considered due to the sub-acute history and respiratory findings, though the abscess was not typical of a 'cold' tuberculous abscess. Disseminated fungal infection was deemed unlikely.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The patient was initially treated empirically with ceftriaxone and gentamicin. After 48 hours, upon suspicion of melioidosis based on culture results, treatment was switched to ceftazidime. He improved after 7 days, became afebrile by day 14, and was then transitioned to oral co-trimoxazole to complete a 20-week course. His glycaemic control was also established. He was discharged after 21 days of hospitalization to continue the 5-month oral eradication therapy and remains well.", "final_diagnosis": "The patient was diagnosed with melioidosis, presenting as septicemia with a back abscess and pneumonia. The diagnosis was confirmed by the isolation of Burkholderia pseudomallei from both pus culture from the abscess and blood cultures. The clinical picture of sepsis with disseminated infection in a patient with uncontrolled diabetes and relevant occupational exposure in an endemic region was highly consistent with this diagnosis.", "disease_name_short": "Melioidosis", "source_filename": "32---A-44-Year-Old-Male-Farmer-from-Laos-With-Di_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Trop.json"}

{"patient_information": "A 42-year-old German man with an unremarkable past medical history.", "chief_complaint": "A 6-day history of fever up to 40\u00b0C, arthralgias, and retro-orbital pain.", "history_of_present_illness": "A 42-year-old man presented to a hospital in Germany with a 6-day history of fever reaching 40\u00b0C (104\u00b0F), accompanied by arthralgias and retro-orbital pain. His symptoms started while traveling, and he presented the day after returning from a 10-week trip to Thailand.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient had just returned from a 10-week trip to Thailand. He spent two months on Little Koh Chang in the Andaman Sea, followed by five days in Hua Hin and five days in Bangkok. He did not take any antimalarial chemoprophylaxis, consistent with national recommendations for his travel itinerary.", "vitals": "On examination, his temperature was 39.1\u00b0C (102.4\u00b0F), blood pressure was 156/80 mmHg, pulse was 106 beats per minute, respiratory rate was 16 breaths per minute, and SpO2 was 95% on ambient air.", "physical_exam": "The physical examination was largely unremarkable. The patient was fully alert with a GCS of 15/15. There was no skin rash or lymphadenopathy. His chest was clear on auscultation, and the abdominal examination revealed no tenderness or organomegaly.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "Initial laboratory results showed thrombocytopenia of 81 x 10^9/L, an elevated CRP of 55 mg/L, and elevated liver enzymes (AST 62 U/L, ALT 106 U/L). A Dengue NS1 antigen test and a rapid diagnostic test for malaria were both negative. A subsequent thick blood film showed Plasmodium species trophozoites at a density of 920/\u03bcL. The species could not be identified on the thin film, but a species-specific PCR ultimately confirmed a mono-infection with P. knowlesi.", "differential_diagnosis": "Given the clinical syndrome and travel history, the primary differential diagnosis was dengue fever, although the duration of fever was long and the NS1 test was negative. Other considerations included Chikungunya, Zika, influenza, and acute HIV infection. Enteric fever was considered less likely. Malaria was a critical diagnosis to exclude, as a negative rapid diagnostic test is not sufficient to rule it out, especially for non-falciparum species.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The patient was admitted to the infectious diseases ward. Based on the positive blood film suspecting non-falciparum malaria, treatment was started with atovaquone/proguanil 250/100mg, four tablets once daily for three days. The patient recovered rapidly and was discharged on the fourth day.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis was Knowlesi malaria, caused by Plasmodium knowlesi. The diagnosis was confirmed by species-specific PCR after an initial thick blood film showed Plasmodium species trophozoites at 920/\u03bcL. This was despite a negative malaria rapid diagnostic test and initial difficulty in identifying the species by microscopy, which can be mistaken for P. malariae.", "disease_name_short": "Knowlesi malaria", "source_filename": "92---A-42-Year-Old-Traveller-Returning-from-Thaila_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Tr.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "A 43-year-old German man with an unremarkable past medical history.", "chief_complaint": "Recurrent fever for three days.", "history_of_present_illness": "The patient complains of recurrent fever for the past three days, with a temperature as high as 39.8\u00b0C (103.6\u00b0F) the day before presentation. He also reports some headache and diarrhoea. Approximately three weeks prior, while in Mozambique, he experienced fever and self-treated for presumed malaria with atovaquone/proguanil from April 27th to 29th.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient is a traveller who was in Mozambique from April 16th to April 30th, followed by a trip to Chile from April 30th to May 17th. He did not take any malaria chemoprophylaxis while in Mozambique. He reports freshwater contact in a small lake near Maputo, Mozambique.", "vitals": "On presentation, the patient was febrile with a temperature of 39.2\u00b0C (102.6\u00b0F). The previous day, his temperature had reached 39.8\u00b0C (103.6\u00b0F).", "physical_exam": "The physical examination was normal, with no rash, hepatomegaly, splenomegaly, or lymphadenopathy noted.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "Initial laboratory results showed a normal total white blood cell count (9.0 x 10^9/L) but significant eosinophilia (2.1 x 10^9/L). C-reactive protein was elevated at 57.9 mg/L, and liver enzymes (AST, ALT, GGT) and LDH were slightly raised. Thick films for Plasmodium species were negative. A chest radiograph and CT scan revealed nodular lesions (2-5mm) in the periphery of the lower lung zones bilaterally. Initial microscopy of three stool and urine samples for Schistosoma eggs were negative, as were initial antischistosomal antibody tests (enzyme-immunoassay and IFA). However, four weeks later, antischistosomal antibodies became positive (IFA 1:1280; cercarial and egg-ELISA positive), and Schistosoma mansoni ova were found in the stool.", "differential_diagnosis": "The primary differential diagnosis for fever in a returned traveler from Mozambique was malaria, especially given the lack of prophylaxis. Other considerations included typhoid fever and amoebic liver abscess. Viral infections like hepatitis, EBV, and CMV were considered due to elevated liver enzymes, but the absence of lymphocytosis made them less likely. The pronounced eosinophilia, however, strongly suggested a helminth infection. Acute schistosomiasis (Katayama syndrome) was the most likely diagnosis given the combination of fever, eosinophilia, and a history of freshwater exposure. Acute fascioliasis was also a rare possibility.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The patient underwent diagnostic investigations. After initial tests for malaria and schistosomiasis were negative, repeat testing was performed. Four weeks after presentation, schistosomiasis was confirmed by positive serology and the detection of ova in the stool. The patient was subsequently treated with praziquantel.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis is acute schistosomiasis (Katayama syndrome) caused by Schistosoma mansoni. This was established by the delayed appearance of positive antischistosomal antibodies (IFA 1:1280 and positive ELISA) and the eventual identification of S. mansoni ova in the stool, four weeks after initial negative tests. The clinical picture of fever, profound eosinophilia, nodular pulmonary lesions, and a clear history of freshwater exposure in Mozambique strongly supported this diagnosis.", "disease_name_short": "Acute schistosomiasis", "source_filename": "20---A-43-Year-Old-Male-Traveller-Returning-from-M_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Tr.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "A 33-year-old male refugee from Afghanistan.", "chief_complaint": "A 6-month history of recurrent fever, night sweats, and progressive back pain.", "history_of_present_illness": "The patient began feeling ill approximately 6 months prior to presentation while living in a refugee camp in Iran. His symptoms included recurrent fever, night sweats, and progressive back pain. He received treatment with tablets and intragluteal injections for several weeks, which led to a temporary subsidence of symptoms, but they recurred after the treatment ended. All medical documents were lost during his subsequent journey to Germany.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient is a refugee from Afghanistan, a country where spinal brucellosis is highly endemic. He lived in a refugee camp in Iran before traveling through Turkey and Eastern Europe to reach Germany four months prior to presentation.", "vitals": "Upon presentation, the patient was afebrile and his vital signs were normal.", "physical_exam": "Physical examination revealed marked spinal tenderness in the lumbar region. The remainder of the examination was unremarkable.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "Laboratory tests showed normal differential blood count, liver and renal function, and LDH. Inflammatory markers were slightly raised, with a C-reactive protein of 26 mg/L (<5 mg/L) and an erythrocyte sedimentation rate of 40 mm/hour (<20 mm/hour). Urinalysis was unremarkable. An MRI of the spine revealed signal enhancement of the L3 and L4 vertebral bodies, the corresponding vertebral disc, and the right psoas muscle, with disc space narrowing and irregularities of the adjacent vertebral endplates. A chest x-ray showed no abnormalities. Several blood cultures and sputum samples for acid-fast bacilli were negative. A CT-guided biopsy of the spinal lesion was performed, and culture of the tissue sample grew Brucella melitensis. An echocardiogram showed no signs of endocarditis.", "differential_diagnosis": "The primary differential diagnosis for infectious spondylitis in this patient included tuberculosis, which is the main cause globally, and spinal brucellosis, which is highly prevalent in his country of origin, Afghanistan. Other less likely infectious causes considered were Staphylococcus aureus, streptococci, and Gram-negative bacteria such as Salmonella species.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The patient's condition was classified as complicated brucellosis with spondylitis and para-vertebral abscess formation. He was treated with a 16-week course of doxycycline 100 mg twice daily and rifampicin 900mg daily. Additionally, he received intramuscular streptomycin (1 g daily) for the first 3 weeks. His fever subsided within several days, and inflammatory markers returned to normal several weeks after initiating therapy. A follow-up MRI at the end of treatment showed full resolution of the paravertebral abscess. One year after completing treatment, the patient showed no signs of relapse.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis is complicated brucellosis with spondylitis of L3/L4 and a paravertebral abscess. This diagnosis was definitively confirmed by the isolation of the causative agent, Brucella melitensis, from a culture of a CT-guided biopsy tissue sample from the affected vertebra. The clinical presentation of chronic fever and back pain, imaging findings of spondylodiscitis, and the patient's epidemiological background from a highly endemic region strongly supported this diagnosis over alternatives like spinal tuberculosis.", "disease_name_short": "Brucellosis", "source_filename": "81---A-33-Year-Old-Refugee-from-Afghanistan-With-_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Tro.json"}

{"patient_information": "A 6-year-old boy from Malawi who is known to be HIV-positive but is not yet on antiretroviral therapy (ART).", "chief_complaint": "High fever, dyspnoea, and a dry cough for 3 days, with subsequent impaired consciousness.", "history_of_present_illness": "The illness began 3 days prior to review with high fever, dyspnoea, and a dry cough. He was admitted the night before and presented with impaired consciousness. A fine maculopapular rash, not previously described, was noted on examination behind his ears and on his forehead. Within the next 3 days, the rash spread to cover his trunk, and skin scaling started over his chest.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient is a boy from Malawi, being treated in a Malawian Central Hospital. No other specific exposures are detailed.", "vitals": "On examination, the patient had a temperature of 39.8\u00b0C (103.6\u00b0F) and laboured breathing.", "physical_exam": "Examination revealed a sick child with impaired consciousness, evidenced by a Blantyre coma score of 3/5. He presented with coryza, bilateral conjunctivitis, and bilateral axillary lymphadenopathy. A fine maculopapular rash was present behind both ears and on the forehead.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "Malaria smears were performed on admission and were found to be negative.", "differential_diagnosis": "The initial differential diagnosis was broad and included malaria, typhoid fever, atypical pneumonia, and various viral infections. However, the presence of the classic triad of cough, coryza, and conjunctivitis, combined with a rash starting behind the ears, made measles the primary suspicion. Other viral causes like rubella and enterovirus, and bacterial infections such as scarlet fever and meningococcal disease, were also considered.", "management_and_clinical_course": "Presumptive antimalarial treatment was started on admission but was stopped after malaria smears were confirmed to be negative. The patient required IV fluids and was given vitamin A for corneal protection. He was isolated due to the contagious nature of his illness. He developed severe pneumonia and received antibiotic treatment. After recovery, antiretroviral therapy was initiated in the out-patient clinic.", "final_diagnosis": "The diagnosis is measles in an immunocompromised child. This is based on the classic clinical presentation featuring the triad of cough, coryza, and conjunctivitis, followed by a characteristic maculopapular rash starting behind the ears and spreading caudally with subsequent desquamation. The diagnosis is further supported by the development of severe complications, including pneumonia and likely encephalitis (given the impaired consciousness), which are common in HIV-positive individuals. Malaria was ruled out by negative smears.", "disease_name_short": "Measles", "source_filename": "31---A-6-Year-Old-Boy-from-Malawi-With-Fever--Cou_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Tro.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "The patient is a 37-year-old woman with an unremarkable past medical history. She does not drink alcohol and an HIV test conducted 3 months prior was negative.", "chief_complaint": "Vomiting blood three times over the past 24 hours.", "history_of_present_illness": "The patient presented with three episodes of vomiting bright red blood over the past 24 hours. She reported no epigastric pain, no previous history of vomiting, no fever or abnormal bleeding, and her stool had been normal in colour. Before the onset of symptoms, she was well and had not taken any regular painkillers.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient is from the Lower Shire Valley in southern Malawi, where she lives and works on a large sugar plantation. The region is highly endemic for schistosomiasis, with most water bodies infested with Schistosoma mansoni and S. haematobium.", "vitals": "Blood pressure was 90/60 mmHg, pulse was 110 bpm, and respiratory rate was 28 breaths per minute. The patient was afebrile.", "physical_exam": "On examination, the patient was slim with slightly pale conjunctivae but was not jaundiced. The abdomen was non-tender and not distended, with the spleen palpable 10cm below the left costal margin and a slightly enlarged liver. There were no stigmata of chronic liver disease, no shifting dullness, and no peripheral oedema. Her lymph nodes were not enlarged, and the rest of the physical examination was normal.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "Laboratory results on admission showed pancytopenia with a WBC of 2.8 x 10\u2079/L, haemoglobin of 8.3 g/dL (normocytic anaemia), and low platelets. Hepatitis B and C serologies were negative. A stool sample for S. mansoni ova was negative. Endoscopy confirmed the presence of oesophageal varices. An ultrasound of the liver revealed a pattern described as typical of pipestem fibrosis.", "differential_diagnosis": "The primary differential was upper gastrointestinal bleeding from oesophageal varices due to portal hypertension, as suggested by splenomegaly and pancytopenia. Hepatosplenic schistosomiasis was considered the most likely cause of portal hypertension due to the patient's residence in an endemic area. Other causes of bleeding like peptic ulcer disease were less likely as they would not explain the splenomegaly. Visceral leishmaniasis was also considered but was less probable due to the absence of fever and its rarity in Southern Africa.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The patient received intravenous fluids and was transferred for endoscopy, where band ligation of the oesophageal varices was performed. She was treated with a single dose of praziquantel 40mg/kg and was subsequently discharged home.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis was hepatosplenic schistosomiasis causing portal hypertension and bleeding from oesophageal varices. This was based on the clinical presentation of haematemesis, marked splenomegaly, and pancytopenia in a patient from a highly endemic region. The diagnosis was supported by an ultrasound showing a pattern typical of pipestem fibrosis, while other causes like viral hepatitis were ruled out. Endoscopy confirmed oesophageal varices as the source of the bleeding.", "disease_name_short": "Hepatosplenic schistosomiasis", "source_filename": "57---A-37-Year-Old-Woman-from-Malawi-With-H_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Tropical-.json"}
---
{"patient_information": "A 35-year-old Malawian woman, who is divorced with three children. She works as a small-scale farmer. The patient is noted to be wasted, with a body mass index of 17 kg/m\u00b2. She is newly diagnosed with HIV.", "chief_complaint": "Fever for three days and progressive weakness over the past several months.", "history_of_present_illness": "The patient presented with a 3-day history of fever and progressive weakness that had worsened over several months. She reported some weight loss but denied cough, night sweats, diarrhoea, dysuria, or any history of abnormal bleeding. Two months prior, she sought care at a local health centre for her weakness, was found to be clinically pale, and was prescribed iron tablets, which she took without improvement as the weakness progressed.", "exposure_and_epidemiology": "The patient is from Malawi and works as a small-scale farmer, selling vegetables at the local market. She lives in a high-prevalence setting for HIV in sub-Saharan Africa. Her three children are well and tested negative for HIV, but her ex-husband refused testing.", "vitals": "On admission, her blood pressure was 90/60 mmHg, pulse was 110 bpm, temperature was 37.8\u00b0C (100\u00b0F), respiratory rate was 25 breaths per minute, and oxygen saturation was 97% on ambient air. Her Glasgow Coma Scale was 15/15.", "physical_exam": "The patient appeared wasted. Her conjunctivae were very pale, but there was no jaundice. Examination of the mouth was normal, with no oral thrush, Kaposi\u2019s sarcoma lesions, or oral hairy leukoplakia. Her chest was clear. The abdomen was soft with slight diffuse tenderness but no guarding, and the spleen was palpable 3 cm below the left costal margin. The rectal examination was normal.", "labs_and_diagnostics": "A malaria rapid diagnostic test was negative. HIV serology was positive. Her full blood count showed a white blood cell count of 3.0 x 10\u2079/L and severe anaemia with a haemoglobin of 4.8 mg/dL. Blood cultures grew Salmonella enterica var Typhimurium. Tuberculosis screening revealed prominent hilar lymph nodes.", "differential_diagnosis": "The primary suspected diagnosis was invasive non-typhoidal Salmonellae (iNTS) infection, given the septic presentation without a clear focus, new HIV diagnosis, abdominal tenderness, and splenomegaly in a sub-Saharan African setting. Enteric fever was considered but deemed less likely as it is less common than iNTS in HIV-positive adults in the region. Tuberculosis was also high on the differential list due to wasting, severe anaemia, and advanced immunosuppression. Visceral leishmaniasis was considered due to fever and pancytopenia but was less likely as the patient lacked gross hepatosplenomegaly and the disease is not endemic in Malawi.", "management_and_clinical_course": "The patient was initiated on intravenous ceftriaxone 2 g once daily and fluid resuscitation. Following blood culture results, her antibiotic regimen was switched to oral ciprofloxacin 500 mg three times a day for 14 days. Due to findings on TB screening, she was also started on antituberculous treatment and antiretroviral therapy (ART). Prophylactically, she received co-trimoxazole and vitamin B6. After a 4-week hospital stay, she was discharged. At a 3-month follow-up in the ART clinic, she was feeling much better, had gained weight, and her haemoglobin levels were improving.", "final_diagnosis": "The final diagnosis was invasive non-typhoidal Salmonella (iNTS) infection, caused by Salmonella enterica var Typhimurium, which was confirmed by blood culture. This infection presented as sepsis in the context of a new diagnosis of advanced HIV infection. The patient's severe anaemia and wasting were attributed to the combined effects of HIV, iNTS, and concomitant tuberculosis, which was diagnosed based on radiological findings of prominent hilar lymph nodes.", "disease_name_short": "Invasive non-typhoidal Salmonella (iNTS) infection", "source_filename": "29---A-35-Year-Old-Woman-from-Malawi-With-Feve_2022_Clinical-Cases-in-Tropic.json"}
